 "annotations": [
  {
   "id": "n-land",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-land",
   "text": "First, because it is the first thing anyone meets. Built from real components at their real size \u2014 fragments, never whole screens \u2014 and on the 1440 board from the WEB app's components, because v1 is a web app and a laptop reader must see the tables and the dashboard they will actually get. `LandMotion` draws the pointer states, because a static artboard cannot show motion and the build cannot implement from a description. Two image slots are specified and not filled \u2014 `docs/design/image-brief.md` carries the shot list, the licences and the reject list."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-opts",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-opts",
   "text": "<b>Three answers to one question. Pick one \u2014 or one and a half.</b> The arc gauge is not merely an inefficient chart at 1440; it answers a question nobody asks at a desk. <i>Can I spend this, right now?</i> is asked standing in a shop, and a dial is a good answer to it. At a laptop, eleven days into a cycle, the question is <i>am I on pace, and what is pulling me off it?</i> \u2014 and a dial has no memory of yesterday and no opinion about tomorrow. <b>The gauge stays at 360</b>, where it is right. Every figure on these three boards is derived from the nineteen dated expenses in `docs/seed-data.md`; the derivations are logged in open-items \u00a7L for the seed script to assert. <b>A</b> is the cycle over time, <b>B</b> is the categories against their pace, <b>C</b> is the month as a shape. My own recommendation is on the reply, not on the board."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-w-in",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-w-in",
   "text": "Welcome, the six onboarding steps, and the whole account flow \u2014 <b>every step on its own artboard</b>, because a six-step flow drawn as one picture is a picture, not a flow. <b>These are WEB screens.</b> In v1 the only place to sign up is the web app; the Expo mobile app is v2 and none of this is drawn for it yet. Sign-up carries the trust panel (\u00a7I8), which since 25 September holds what came off the landing page: encryption, where the keys live, and the caveat that movement reaches the server before it is encrypted. <i>Forgot password</i> and <i>Reset password</i> are now three screens, not one \u2014 ask, wait, set \u2014 because the step in the middle is where resets actually fail."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-w-home",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-w-home",
   "text": "Home and its five states. The width holds the workings: the figure and today's chart left, the reason \u2014 where the money is, then every category worst-first \u2014 right, and reading one against the other is exactly what 360 cannot do. Each state is its own board because each has its own fix, and a row of five made them look like a set of variations rather than five different screens someone will actually be looking at."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-w-money",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-w-money",
   "text": "The month's money on a laptop. Transactions gets a standing filter rail and real columns \u2014 stacked filters scroll the list away, and losing your place is the one thing that matters here. Debts loses its tabs entirely, because the best thing width buys is sometimes one less control. Both empty states of Transactions are separate boards: one wants a movement, the other wants the filters cleared."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-w-acct",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-w-acct",
   "text": "What an account is for, and the three screens the paid tier exists to serve. <b>There is no lock anywhere on this page</b> \u2014 no padlock, no grey-out, no crown, and the word <i>unlock</i> appears nowhere (item 24, and the build guard checks it). Reconciliation is one movement at a time, never a list of forms. The household disagreement is two people agreeing, not software refereeing: both figures drawn as equals, no rose, and <i>leave it for now</i> the same weight as the other two."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-m-in",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-m-in",
   "text": "The same web app in a phone browser. Field widths, copy, order, validation and the step counter are identical to 1440 \u2014 the desktop layout is one rule, not six designs \u2014 so an implementer builds one component and swaps the frame at the breakpoint. The three picker boards draw what steps 2 and 3 referred to and never showed."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-m-home",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-m-home",
   "text": "Home at 360, its states, and the More sheet \u2014 which is 360-only, because at 1440 everything in it is a first-class sidebar item. Every figure is one thumb-reach from the thing it describes."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-m-money",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-m-money",
   "text": "The month's money in a phone browser, one hand, bad connection assumed. Tabs earn their place at this width where they did not at 1440. Opening balances and debts are dated 24 September, the day before the cycle, so they never count as this cycle's saving."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-m-acct",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-m-acct",
   "text": "Settings, and the import flow \u2014 spec \u00a77.9, the most dangerous action in the app. Every refusal ends with \u201cNothing has changed.\u201d Refusals are neutral, never red: the file was wrong, the person was not."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-brand",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-brand",
   "text": "The mark is the mizan beam \u2014 a set of scales, tipped, because the app exists to say whether today is in balance. Locked. It is the app icon, the favicon, the sidebar lockup and the letterhead on the printed debt record."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-found",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-found",
   "text": "The primitives sheet \u2014 every component in every state, both themes \u2014 then the palette and type tile, and the printed debt record: A4, black and white, no app chrome, because it leaves the app and is read by someone who was never in it."
  }
 ],
